                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "If I could give this game a review of \"mixed\", I would. For clarity, this is a \"Not Recommended\" review because there is obviously no \"Mixed\" option but also because I lean that direction for the reasons I will describe below.\n\nFor a bit of history, I backed this game on Indiegogo in 2015. It made $10,148 from 140 backers, which was not enough to meet the stated funding goal, but because they used the \"flexible funding\" option backers were charged anyway and received their rewards. I backed the game because there was a playable demo on their Indiegogo page which I felt had a lot of promise (and I was also at the age where the gooner content was closer to a plus than a minus).\n\nWhen I played it at release, I really enjoyed the game, beating it all the way through on Normal difficulty. That version of me definitely would have given the game a Recommended review. However, having replayed it ten years later on Hard difficulty, the cracks have begun to show where they did not before.\n\nThere is a serious issue with hidden information in this game. For one, there is no real hints or indication as to where collectibles and other secrets are. There are many occasions where you are incentivized to carpet bomb an area with carrots to find hidden breakable platforms because the game provides no visual indicators. However, not every secret is revealable with bombs; sometimes they'll throw a curveball and expect you to use magic with, again, no indication whatsoever. As a result, you either have to use a guide or bomb, shoot, and rub yourself against every wall and surface to find the various collectibles in the game.\n\nThe combat feels super shallow as a result of its formula. Your attack variety is ultimately meaningless because there are no real combos in the game; you hit the enemy with a basic combo until your stamina meter depletes and you hold the charge shot button and release it whenever you're pointing at an enemy. This applies to both basic enemies and bosses, and the only time you can even attack the bosses (besides pot shots with magic) are in-between attack patterns when they're sitting ducks. Furthermore, maybe it's just a feature of bullet hell games, but I feel like there are too many times where bosses use unreactable attack patterns that are functionally mandatory damage until you memorize the cue. Don't get me wrong, I can handle learning attack patterns, but for those boss attacks to *just* be memorization without a significant skill component feels cheap. I also understand why Erina's hurtbox is much smaller than her character model, but it ends up being visually confusing, especially early on.\n\nThere's a temporary buff mechanic available in Rabi Rabi Town with the people you recruit that you can use by paying them a certain amount of money. Frankly, I really wish they did not. It's something you could get away with never touching, but the concept is heavily flawed from the outset. It encourages you to mindlessly grind for cash in-between difficult bosses to optimize your chances (as does restocking on healing items, to a lesser extent).\n\nOne thing that is pretty cool about Rabi-Ribi is their encouragement of sequence breaking. I do find it interesting in games when it's possible to access far-away areas and beat late-game bosses early using obscure technical movement, but what bothers me is that Rabi-Ribi's \"obscure technical movement\" is just regular platforming abilities that they don't give you a tutorial for. It's cool to discover at first, but when you really think about it it's just lazy.\n\nThen, of course, there's the obvious: the gooner slop character designs genuinely do make it hard to take this game seriously. It is an active hinderance to enjoyment of the game's plot which is, in isolation, mildly interesting at times. The dialogue isn't good either, drawing inspiration from *that* genre of anime the character designs are derived from.\n\nThe music is great and, as I felt when I backed the game in 2015, there is promise in the premise and mechanics. I just don't think it makes good on that promise in the way it needed to.",
